## Recommended: `System.IO.Abstractions` NuGet package

This is the established, battle-tested solution for making file system calls testable — no need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string ReadConfig(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void WriteConfig(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.ReadConfig("/config.json"));

service.WriteConfig("/output.json", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/output.json"));

Assert.False(service.EnsureDirectory("/nonexistent"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the entire `System.IO` surface (`File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream`, etc.), so you won't need to extend it as your usage grows. The `MockFileSystem` provides an in-memory filesystem out of the box — no mocking framework required.

**Migration tip:** Replace `File.` → `fileSystem.File.` and `Directory.` → `fileSystem.Directory.` at each call site after injecting `IFileSystem`. If you'd like help bulk-migrating call sites, let me know.
